<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raden Patah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1455–1518</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Raden Patah was the founder and first sultan of Demak, the first Muslim sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, in the late 15th century. Javanese tradition makes him a son of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> king and a Chinese mother. Supported by the Muslim saints known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wali-songo">Wali Songo</a>, he made Demak the leading power on Java's north coast as Majapahit declined.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raden-patah.jpg" alt="Raden Patah" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mhatopzz · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MUS_Koin_Sultan_Pangeran_Raden_Fatah.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Raden Patah was known by many names, among them Raden Bagus Kasan and, in the Chinese chronicle of the Sam Poo Kong temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semarang">Semarang</a>, Jin Bun, a name without a surname because only his mother was Chinese. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babad-tanah-jawi">Babad Tanah Jawi</a> he was the son of Brawijaya V, the last king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>, by a Chinese concubine, the daughter of Kyai Batong; she later married Arya Damar, and their son Raden Kusen was Raden Patah's half-brother. The Sam Poo Kong chronicle names his father Kung-ta-bu-mi, Bhre Kertabhumi, and places his mother's second marriage with Swan Liong in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a>. The Sejarah Banten gives a different founder altogether.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babad-tanah-jawi">Babad Tanah Jawi</a> relates that he refused to succeed Arya Damar as Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a> and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> with Raden Kusen, where both studied under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunan-ampel">Sunan Ampel</a> in Surabaya. Raden Patah then cleared the Glagahwangi forest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a> for a pesantren.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Brawijaya, fearing a rebellion, had him summoned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>, acknowledged him as his son and made him regent; Glagahwangi was renamed Demak, with its capital at Bintara. The Chinese chronicle has him move to Demak in 1475 and take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semarang">Semarang</a> in 1477. In 1479 he inaugurated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-demak">Great Mosque of Demak</a> as the centre of government, and he introduced the Salokantara as a royal statute book. The babad accounts tell of war with Majapahit after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunan-ampel">Sunan Ampel</a>, and the Chinese chronicle dates the storming of the Majapahit capital to 1478. Krom, in Javaansche Geschiedenis, and Yamin, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a>, argued instead that it was Girindrawardhana who attacked Majapahit in 1478, a view supported by the Petak inscription.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tome-pires">Tomé Pires</a> reported in his Suma Oriental that Pate Rodim, identified with Raden Patah, inaugurated the renovated mosque of Demak in 1507. In 1512 Pate Unus, regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jepara">Jepara</a>, attacked the Portuguese at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>; Portuguese and Chinese sources agree that the Demak fleet was destroyed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to the Chinese chronicle Raden Patah died in 1518 at the age of 63 and was succeeded by Yat Sun, called Pangeran Sabrang Lor in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babad-tanah-jawi">Babad Tanah Jawi</a>. His titles varied by source, Sultan Syah Alam Akbar in the Serat Pranitiradya and Sultan Surya Alam in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hikayat-banjar">Hikayat Banjar</a>. The historian Merle Calvin Ricklefs distinguished him from a Muslim named Cek-ko-po, most likely from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, who may have founded the sultanate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Raden Patah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the late 15th century its ruler <b>Raden Patah</b>, described in Javanese tradition as a son of the last Majapahit king and a Chinese mother, threw off Majapahit's overlordship.</blockquote><a href="/state/demak-sultanate">Demak Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jepara">Jepara</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demak-sultanate">Sultanate of Demak</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-6.57,110.68&amp;year=1495" aria-label="Jepara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7615 2409 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Raden Patah"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8065 2679h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8065 2679h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-6.57,110.68&amp;year=1495">What was here in 1495? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
